<?xml version="1.0"?>
<system name="tecs"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://jsbsim.sourceforge.net/JSBSimSystem.xsd">

<!-- ===================================================================
     Total Energy Control System, plus lateral guidance.

     Lives in JSBSim XML rather than Python (ASSUMPTIONS §5 Phase 2) so it
     runs at FDM rate, travels with the aircraft model, and reproduces
     identically in the headless and embedded hosts. Python owns only the
     slower guidance layer, which writes the ap/*-setpoint-* properties this
     file consumes every step.

     WHY TECS AND NOT TWO PID LOOPS  (§2.3)
     ======================================
     Altitude and airspeed are not independently controllable with elevator
     and throttle as separate SISO loops. The plant

         [ hdot ]   [ dhdot/dde   dhdot/ddT ] [ de ]
         [ Vdot ] = [ dVdot/dde   dVdot/ddT ] [ dT ]

     has off-diagonal terms comparable to the diagonal ones, so decentralised
     control of it produces cross-coupled oscillation. That is exactly what
     the previous build shipped: commanded 300 m and 233 kt, the altitude
     descended monotonically to 52 m low and still diverging while the
     airspeed overshot by 18 kt and then decayed back through its target.

     TECS changes variables instead. Specific total energy

         STE  = g*h + V^2/2          -> commanded with THROTTLE
         SEB  = g*h - V^2/2          -> commanded with PITCH  (energy balance)

     is approximately diagonal in (throttle, pitch), so the two loops stop
     fighting. One time constant tau governs both, which is the structural
     point: not two independently-tuned bandwidths that can disagree.

     NORMALISATION
     =============
     Both energy=rate errors are divided by (g * V), not by (g * tau).
     ASSUMPTIONS §6.4 writes the latter, but it is dimensionally an
     acceleration, whereas what the pitch loop needs is an ANGLE. Since a
     specific potential energy rate is g*hdot and a flight path angle is
     hdot/V, dividing an energy rate by (g*V) yields radians directly.

     This was not a cosmetic difference. With the /(g*tau) form the pitch
     command sat pegged at its +15/=10 degree limits almost continuously and
     the altitude hold oscillated bang=bang at about +/=13 m.

     MEASUREMENT  (§1.3, §6.4)
     =========================
     Nothing here differentiates a raw altitude or airspeed measurement.
     Climb rate is JSBSim's own state-derived h-dot, and true airspeed rate is
     computed from the body-axis state derivatives:

         Vdot = (u*udot + v*vdot + w*wdot) / V

     which is exact for the airspeed magnitude and comes from the same
     accelerations the load factor does.

     ANTI-WINDUP
     ===========
     Every integrating loop has its <trigger> wired to a saturation flag for
     its own actuator, which is JSBSim's built-in mechanism for freezing an
     integrator. The flag is computed from the pre-clip command, so it reports
     that the controller *wanted* more authority than it has.
     =================================================================== -->

<!-- ========== engage / setpoints, written by the guidance layer ======== -->
<property value="0">ap/enable</property>
<property value="0">ap/altitude-setpoint-ft</property>
<property value="0">ap/tas-setpoint-fps</property>
<property value="0">ap/heading-setpoint-deg</property>
<!-- Direct bank command, selected by ap/bank-mode. Heading hold is the normal
     path; commanding bank directly is what §6.5's bank-tracking criterion
     actually asks for, and characterising the roll loop through a heading step
     measures the guidance law instead. -->
<property value="0">ap/bank-mode</property>
<property value="0">ap/bank-setpoint-rad</property>

<!-- ========== trim references, captured at engage ====================== -->
<property value="0">ap/trim/throttle</property>
<!-- Held at the trimmed control positions so a disengaged autopilot leaves
     the actuators exactly where trim put them, rather than reading back its
     own output. Captured at engage by the guidance layer. -->
<property value="0">ap/tecs/throttle-passthrough</property>
<property value="0">ap/pitch/elevator-passthrough</property>
<property value="0">ap/lat/aileron-passthrough</property>
<property value="0">ap/yaw/rudder-passthrough</property>
<property value="0">ap/trim/pitch-rad</property>
<!-- Trimmed surface positions. The inner loops command a DEVIATION from these,
     not an absolute deflection. Without them, engaging drives every surface to
     zero on the first frame: the trimmed B747 elevator sits at =0.055, so
     engaging dropped it to 0 and the aircraft dipped 15 m before the loop
     recovered. That dip is an engage transient of the controller's own making,
     which is the §1.1 failure in miniature. -->
<property value="0">ap/trim/elevator</property>
<property value="0">ap/trim/aileron</property>
<property value="0">ap/trim/rudder</property>

<!-- ==== control sign convention, measured per airframe ================
     JSBSim imposes no sign convention on fcs/*-cmd-norm; each aircraft's own
     flight_control section decides. On the B747 a positive elevator command
     pitches the nose DOWN and a positive rudder command yaws LEFT. A
     controller that hardcodes one convention does not fail loudly on an
     airframe using the other, it closes the loop with positive feedback: the
     first run of this file pitched down through 2676 m and hit NaN in 89 s.
     core.control.signs measures these and writes them at engage. =====
     +1 means a positive command produces a positive body=axis rate. -->
<property value="1.0">ap/sign/elevator</property>
<property value="1.0">ap/sign/aileron</property>
<property value="1.0">ap/sign/rudder</property>

<!-- ========== tuning =================================================== -->
<!-- tau: the single time constant governing both energy loops (§6.4). It sets
     the demanded rates directly, hdot_dem = (h_dem = h)/tau, which is where a
     time constant belongs. -->
<property value="5.0">ap/tecs/tau</property>
<!-- Wv in [0,2]: 0 = height priority, 1 = balanced, 2 = speed priority.
     Wh is 2-Wv, so the pair always sums to 2 (ArduPilot TECS_SPDWEIGHT). -->
<property value="1.0">ap/tecs/speed-weight</property>
        <!-- alt error -> hdot demand, 1/s -->
        <!-- speed error -> vdot demand, 1/s -->
<property value="40.0">ap/tecs/hdot-max-fps</property>
<property value="6.0">ap/tecs/vdot-max-fps2</property>
<!-- Package D: the throttle gains are NORMALISED by the airframe's measured
     thrust range. ap/tecs/ste-error is already a required thrust-to-weight
     increment (an energy-rate error over g*V); multiplying it by
     thr-per-ste = 1 / ((T_max - T_idle)/W), measured at engage by
     core.performance, gives the throttle increment that produces exactly
     that thrust. With it, kt-p is a dimensionless loop gain: 1.0 means
     "command the throttle the energy error requires"; the same
     gains give the same bandwidth on every airframe. Before this, a fixed
     1.2 drove the B747 to 17-54% of its excess power and the c172p to 98%
     throttle (measured). The 1.0 default here is overwritten at engage from
     a measurement; a guard makes sure it is. -->
<property value="1.0">ap/tecs/thr-per-ste</property>
<property value="1.0">ap/tecs/kt-p</property>
<property value="0.10">ap/tecs/kt-i</property>
<!-- Measured specific excess power at full throttle / deficit at idle, fps,
     written at engage; hdot-max is derived from the first so the demanded
     climb rate is one the airframe can deliver at constant speed. -->
<property value="0">ap/tecs/stedot-max-fps</property>
<property value="0">ap/tecs/stedot-min-fps</property>
<!-- Package D: with the throttle loop normalised, the energy-DISTRIBUTION
     loop became the measured bottleneck: at ke-p 0.5 a 300 m step left a
     2.9 kt speed surplus untraded, the throttle loop read the total energy
     as satisfied, and the climb stalled at 6.4 m/s of a 12.2 demanded.
     Swept (experiments/airborne/tecs_step.py): ke-p 0.5 gives 43.9 s to
     90%, 1.0 gives 33.7 s, 1.5 gives 30.4 s, 2.0 gives 28.7 s with 0.1 m
     overshoot and a 1.0 kt CAS excursion, climb rate tracking its demand.
     The SEB error is already an angle (radians), so this gain is
     dimensionless and airframe-neutral. -->
<property value="2.0">ap/tecs/ke-p</property>
<property value="0.32">ap/tecs/ke-i</property>
<!-- Turn compensation: drag rises with 1/cos(phi), so a banked turn needs
     more thrust for the same energy state (§6.4, ArduPilot TECS_RLL2THR). -->
<property value="0.20">ap/tecs/roll-to-throttle</property>

<property value="2.5">ap/pitch/k-theta</property>   <!-- pitch error -> q demand -->
<property value="3.0">ap/pitch/kp-q</property>
<property value="0.5">ap/pitch/ki-q</property>
<property value="1.2">ap/lat/k-hdg</property>       <!-- heading error -> bank demand -->
<property value="3.5">ap/lat/k-phi</property>       <!-- bank error -> roll rate demand -->
<property value="2.0">ap/lat/kp-p</property>
<property value="0.05">ap/lat/ki-p</property>
<!-- Yaw damper gain. Raised from 1.5 to 3.0 with the coordinator (Package
     G): swept with it on the B747 at 3000 m / 230 kt, the Dutch-roll
     damping ratio after a rudder pulse is 0.21 at 1.5 and 0.25 at 3.0 at
     the chosen sideslip loop gain, with the turn itself unchanged. -->
<property value="3.0">ap/yaw/k-r</property>
<property value="1.0">ap/yaw/washout-c1</property>  <!-- 1/tau_w, tau_w ~ 1 s -->
<!-- Turn coordination (Package G): rudder per degree of sideslip, SIGNED
     (negative feedback included), and its integral gain. Both are written
     at engage from the beta-per-rudder slope core.control.coordination
     measures on the engaging airframe at its own state; the template
     value 0 is the pre-Package-G yaw channel (damper only), which flew a
     25 deg turn 9% below g tan(phi)/V with 1.08 deg of sideslip. -->
<property value="0.0">ap/yaw/k-beta</property>
<property value="0.0">ap/yaw/ki-beta</property>
<!-- A rudder offset added to the command: the probe input the
     coordination measurement uses (and nothing else writes). -->
<property value="0.0">ap/yaw/rudder-bias</property>

<!-- ========== limits =================================================== -->
<property value="0.436332">ap/limits/bank-rad</property>       <!-- 25 deg -->
<property value="0.261799">ap/limits/pitch-max-rad</property>  <!-- 15 deg -->
<property value="-0.174533">ap/limits/pitch-min-rad</property> <!-- -10 deg -->
<property value="0.174533">ap/limits/q-rad_sec</property>      <!-- 10 deg/s -->
<property value="0.349066">ap/limits/p-rad_sec</property>      <!-- 20 deg/s -->

<!-- ===================================================================
     1. Measurements and energy errors
     =================================================================== -->
<channel name="TECS measurements" execrate="1">

  <!-- True airspeed rate from the body-axis state derivatives. The clamp on
       the denominator keeps this finite on the ground and at zero airspeed;
       it never engages in flight. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/vdot-fps2">
    <function>
      <quotient>
        <sum>
          <product>
            <property>velocities/u-fps</property>
            <property>accelerations/udot-ft_sec2</property>
          </product>
          <product>
            <property>velocities/v-fps</property>
            <property>accelerations/vdot-ft_sec2</property>
          </product>
          <product>
            <property>velocities/w-fps</property>
            <property>accelerations/wdot-ft_sec2</property>
          </product>
        </sum>
        <max>
          <property>velocities/vtrue-fps</property>
          <value>1.0</value>
        </max>
      </quotient>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- Demanded climb rate, proportional to altitude error and limited. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/hdot-demand-fps">
    <function>
      <quotient>
        <difference>
          <property>ap/altitude-setpoint-ft</property>
          <property>position/h-sl-ft</property>
        </difference>
        <property>ap/tecs/tau</property>
      </quotient>
    </function>
    <clipto>
      <min>-ap/tecs/hdot-max-fps</min>
      <max> ap/tecs/hdot-max-fps</max>
    </clipto>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- Demanded longitudinal acceleration, proportional to speed error. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/vdot-demand-fps2">
    <function>
      <quotient>
        <difference>
          <property>ap/tas-setpoint-fps</property>
          <property>velocities/vtrue-fps</property>
        </difference>
        <property>ap/tecs/tau</property>
      </quotient>
    </function>
    <clipto>
      <min>-ap/tecs/vdot-max-fps2</min>
      <max> ap/tecs/vdot-max-fps2</max>
    </clipto>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- True when the demanded climb rate is against its own limit. While that
       holds, the energy error reflects a demand the controller has chosen not
       to pursue rather than authority it lacks, so integrating it winds up an
       integrator that then has to be unwound past the target. Feeding this into
       the anti=windup triggers cut the altitude overshoot from 20.5% to single
       figures. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/hdot-demand-limited">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <gt>
          <abs><property>ap/tecs/hdot-demand-fps</property></abs>
          <product>
            <property>ap/tecs/hdot-max-fps</property>
            <value>0.999</value>
          </product>
        </gt>
        <value>1.0</value>
        <value>0.0</value>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- Specific total energy rate error: what the throttle answers.
       STE_err = g*(hdot_dem - hdot) + V*(Vdot_dem - Vdot), normalised by
       g*tau so the gains below are dimensionless. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/ste-error">
    <function>
      <quotient>
        <sum>
          <product>
            <value>32.174049</value>
            <difference>
              <property>ap/tecs/hdot-demand-fps</property>
              <property>velocities/h-dot-fps</property>
            </difference>
          </product>
          <product>
            <property>velocities/vtrue-fps</property>
            <difference>
              <property>ap/tecs/vdot-demand-fps2</property>
              <property>ap/tecs/vdot-fps2</property>
            </difference>
          </product>
        </sum>
        <product>
          <value>32.174049</value>
          <max>
            <property>velocities/vtrue-fps</property>
            <value>1.0</value>
          </max>
        </product>
      </quotient>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- Specific energy BALANCE rate error: what the pitch answers.
       SEB_err = Wh*g*(hdot_dem - hdot) - Wv*V*(Vdot_dem - Vdot), Wh = 2 - Wv.
       Trading height against speed is precisely what elevator does, which is
       why this is the variable the pitch loop closes on. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/seb-error">
    <function>
      <quotient>
        <difference>
          <product>
            <difference>
              <value>2.0</value>
              <property>ap/tecs/speed-weight</property>
            </difference>
            <value>32.174049</value>
            <difference>
              <property>ap/tecs/hdot-demand-fps</property>
              <property>velocities/h-dot-fps</property>
            </difference>
          </product>
          <product>
            <property>ap/tecs/speed-weight</property>
            <property>velocities/vtrue-fps</property>
            <difference>
              <property>ap/tecs/vdot-demand-fps2</property>
              <property>ap/tecs/vdot-fps2</property>
            </difference>
          </product>
        </difference>
        <product>
          <value>32.174049</value>
          <max>
            <property>velocities/vtrue-fps</property>
            <value>1.0</value>
          </max>
        </product>
      </quotient>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

</channel>

<!-- ===================================================================
     2. Throttle loop == commands specific TOTAL energy
     =================================================================== -->
<channel name="TECS throttle" execrate="1">

  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/throttle-trigger">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <lt><property>ap/enable</property><value>0.5</value></lt>
        <value>-1.0</value>
        <property>ap/tecs/throttle-saturated</property>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- Normalised gains: kt-p and kt-i times the measured throttle-per-
       (dT/W). Computed as properties so the PID reads them each step. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/kt-p-normalised">
    <function>
      <product>
        <property>ap/tecs/kt-p</property>
        <property>ap/tecs/thr-per-ste</property>
      </product>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/kt-i-normalised">
    <function>
      <product>
        <property>ap/tecs/kt-i</property>
        <property>ap/tecs/thr-per-ste</property>
      </product>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <pid name="ap/tecs/throttle-pid">
    <input> ap/tecs/ste-error </input>
    <kp> ap/tecs/kt-p-normalised </kp>
    <ki> ap/tecs/kt-i-normalised </ki>
    <kd> 0.0 </kd>
    <!-- Freeze the integrator whenever the throttle command has hit a stop. -->
    <trigger> ap/tecs/throttle-trigger </trigger>
    <clipto> <min> -1.0 </min> <max> 1.0 </max> </clipto>
  </pid>

  <!-- Extra thrust to hold energy through a turn: drag scales with load
       factor, and load factor in a coordinated turn is 1/cos(phi). -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/turn-compensation">
    <function>
      <product>
        <property>ap/tecs/roll-to-throttle</property>
        <difference>
          <quotient>
            <value>1.0</value>
            <max>
              <abs><cos><property>attitude/phi-rad</property></cos></abs>
              <value>0.2</value>
            </max>
          </quotient>
          <value>1.0</value>
        </difference>
      </product>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- Package D feed-forward: the throttle increment the DEMANDED energy
       rate requires, (g*hdot_dem + V*Vdot_dem)/(g*V) times the measured
       throttle-per-(dT/W). Without it the loop is proportional-only while
       the climb-rate demand sits at its limit (the anti-windup trigger
       freezes the integrator there), and a unity-gain proportional loop on
       a rate settles at HALF the demand: measured, 5.3 m/s achieved for
       12.2 demanded. The feed-forward supplies the demand; P and I correct
       the residual (ArduPilot AP_TECS ff_throttle does the same). -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/throttle-ff">
    <function>
      <product>
        <property>ap/tecs/thr-per-ste</property>
        <quotient>
          <sum>
            <product>
              <value>32.174049</value>
              <property>ap/tecs/hdot-demand-fps</property>
            </product>
            <product>
              <property>velocities/vtrue-fps</property>
              <property>ap/tecs/vdot-demand-fps2</property>
            </product>
          </sum>
          <product>
            <value>32.174049</value>
            <max>
              <property>velocities/vtrue-fps</property>
              <value>1.0</value>
            </max>
          </product>
        </quotient>
      </product>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- Pre-clip command, kept so saturation can be detected honestly. -->
  <summer name="ap/tecs/throttle-raw">
    <input> ap/trim/throttle </input>
    <input> ap/tecs/throttle-ff </input>
    <input> ap/tecs/throttle-pid </input>
    <input> ap/tecs/turn-compensation </input>
  </summer>

  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/throttle-saturated">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <or>
          <lt><property>ap/tecs/throttle-raw</property><value>0.0</value></lt>
          <gt><property>ap/tecs/throttle-raw</property><value>1.0</value></gt>
          <gt><property>ap/tecs/hdot-demand-limited</property><value>0.5</value></gt>
        </or>
        <value>1.0</value>
        <value>0.0</value>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <pure_gain name="ap/tecs/throttle-command">
    <input> ap/tecs/throttle-raw </input>
    <gain> 1.0 </gain>
    <clipto> <min> 0.0 </min> <max> 1.0 </max> </clipto>
  </pure_gain>

  <!-- Only drive the actuator when engaged, so a disengaged autopilot leaves
       the trimmed controls exactly where trim put them. -->
  <!-- One <output> per engine. The list is generated when the derived
       aircraft is built, because engine count is a property of the airframe
       (the B747 has four) and a hand-written list would silently drive only
       some of them. -->
  <switch name="ap/output/throttle">
    <default value="fcs/throttle-cmd-norm"/>
    <test logic="AND" value="ap/tecs/throttle-command">
      ap/enable GT 0.5
    </test>
<!--THROTTLE_OUTPUTS-->
  </switch>

</channel>

<!-- ===================================================================
     3. Pitch loop == commands specific energy BALANCE
     =================================================================== -->
<channel name="TECS pitch" execrate="1">

  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/pitch-trigger">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <lt><property>ap/enable</property><value>0.5</value></lt>
        <value>-1.0</value>
        <property>ap/tecs/pitch-saturated</property>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <pid name="ap/tecs/pitch-pid">
    <input> ap/tecs/seb-error </input>
    <kp> ap/tecs/ke-p </kp>
    <ki> ap/tecs/ke-i </ki>
    <kd> 0.0 </kd>
    <trigger> ap/tecs/pitch-trigger </trigger>
    <clipto> <min> -0.5 </min> <max> 0.5 </max> </clipto>
  </pid>

  <summer name="ap/tecs/pitch-raw-rad">
    <input> ap/trim/pitch-rad </input>
    <input> ap/tecs/pitch-pid </input>
  </summer>

  <fcs_function name="ap/tecs/pitch-saturated">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <or>
          <lt>
            <property>ap/tecs/pitch-raw-rad</property>
            <property>ap/limits/pitch-min-rad</property>
          </lt>
          <gt>
            <property>ap/tecs/pitch-raw-rad</property>
            <property>ap/limits/pitch-max-rad</property>
          </gt>
          <gt><property>ap/tecs/hdot-demand-limited</property><value>0.5</value></gt>
        </or>
        <value>1.0</value>
        <value>0.0</value>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- Clipped BEFORE the attitude loop sees it (§6.4): an unreachable
       command must never be allowed to wind up the inner loop. -->
  <pure_gain name="ap/tecs/pitch-command-rad">
    <input> ap/tecs/pitch-raw-rad </input>
    <gain> 1.0 </gain>
    <clipto>
      <min> ap/limits/pitch-min-rad </min>
      <max> ap/limits/pitch-max-rad </max>
    </clipto>
  </pure_gain>

</channel>

<!-- ===================================================================
     4. Pitch attitude inner loop -> elevator

     Bandwidth is deliberately well above the TECS outer loop: both TECS and
     the lateral guidance assume time-scale separation (§6.5).
     =================================================================== -->
<channel name="Pitch inner loop" execrate="1">

  <fcs_function name="ap/pitch/q-demand">
    <function>
      <product>
        <property>ap/pitch/k-theta</property>
        <difference>
          <property>ap/tecs/pitch-command-rad</property>
          <property>attitude/theta-rad</property>
        </difference>
      </product>
    </function>
    <clipto>
      <min>-ap/limits/q-rad_sec</min>
      <max> ap/limits/q-rad_sec</max>
    </clipto>
  </fcs_function>

  <summer name="ap/pitch/q-error">
    <input>  ap/pitch/q-demand </input>
    <input> -velocities/q-rad_sec </input>
  </summer>

  <fcs_function name="ap/pitch/elevator-trigger">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <lt><property>ap/enable</property><value>0.5</value></lt>
        <value>-1.0</value>
        <property>ap/pitch/elevator-saturated</property>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <pid name="ap/pitch/elevator-pid">
    <input> ap/pitch/q-error </input>
    <kp> ap/pitch/kp-q </kp>
    <ki> ap/pitch/ki-q </ki>
    <kd> 0.0 </kd>
    <trigger> ap/pitch/elevator-trigger </trigger>
    <clipto> <min> -1.0 </min> <max> 1.0 </max> </clipto>
  </pid>

  <fcs_function name="ap/pitch/elevator-saturated">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <gt>
          <abs><property>ap/pitch/elevator-pid</property></abs>
          <value>0.98</value>
        </gt>
        <value>1.0</value>
        <value>0.0</value>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <fcs_function name="ap/pitch/elevator-command">
    <function>
      <sum>
        <property>ap/trim/elevator</property>
        <product>
          <property>ap/sign/elevator</property>
          <property>ap/pitch/elevator-pid</property>
        </product>
      </sum>
    </function>
    <clipto> <min> -1.0 </min> <max> 1.0 </max> </clipto>
  </fcs_function>

  <switch name="ap/output/elevator">
    <default value="fcs/elevator-cmd-norm"/>
    <test logic="AND" value="ap/pitch/elevator-command">
      ap/enable GT 0.5
    </test>
    <output> fcs/elevator-cmd-norm </output>
  </switch>

</channel>

<!-- ===================================================================
     5. Lateral guidance -> bank demand
     =================================================================== -->
<channel name="Lateral guidance" execrate="1">

  <!-- Heading error wrapped to (-pi, pi] via atan2, so a command across
       north takes the short way round instead of a 350-degree turn. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/lat/heading-error-rad">
    <function>
      <atan2>
        <sin>
          <difference>
            <product>
              <property>ap/heading-setpoint-deg</property>
              <value>0.0174532925</value>
            </product>
            <property>attitude/psi-rad</property>
          </difference>
        </sin>
        <cos>
          <difference>
            <product>
              <property>ap/heading-setpoint-deg</property>
              <value>0.0174532925</value>
            </product>
            <property>attitude/psi-rad</property>
          </difference>
        </cos>
      </atan2>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <!-- Clipped before the attitude loop, per §6.4. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/lat/bank-from-heading-rad">
    <function>
      <product>
        <property>ap/lat/k-hdg</property>
        <property>ap/lat/heading-error-rad</property>
      </product>
    </function>
    <clipto>
      <min>-ap/limits/bank-rad</min>
      <max> ap/limits/bank-rad</max>
    </clipto>
  </fcs_function>

  <switch name="ap/lat/bank-demand-rad">
    <default value="ap/lat/bank-from-heading-rad"/>
    <test logic="AND" value="ap/bank-setpoint-rad">
      ap/bank-mode GT 0.5
    </test>
  </switch>

</channel>

<!-- ===================================================================
     6. Roll attitude inner loop -> aileron
     =================================================================== -->
<channel name="Roll inner loop" execrate="1">

  <fcs_function name="ap/lat/p-demand">
    <function>
      <product>
        <property>ap/lat/k-phi</property>
        <difference>
          <property>ap/lat/bank-demand-rad</property>
          <property>attitude/phi-rad</property>
        </difference>
      </product>
    </function>
    <clipto>
      <min>-ap/limits/p-rad_sec</min>
      <max> ap/limits/p-rad_sec</max>
    </clipto>
  </fcs_function>

  <summer name="ap/lat/p-error">
    <input>  ap/lat/p-demand </input>
    <input> -velocities/p-rad_sec </input>
  </summer>

  <fcs_function name="ap/lat/aileron-trigger">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <lt><property>ap/enable</property><value>0.5</value></lt>
        <value>-1.0</value>
        <property>ap/lat/aileron-saturated</property>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <pid name="ap/lat/aileron-pid">
    <input> ap/lat/p-error </input>
    <kp> ap/lat/kp-p </kp>
    <ki> ap/lat/ki-p </ki>
    <kd> 0.0 </kd>
    <trigger> ap/lat/aileron-trigger </trigger>
    <clipto> <min> -1.0 </min> <max> 1.0 </max> </clipto>
  </pid>

  <fcs_function name="ap/lat/aileron-saturated">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <gt>
          <abs><property>ap/lat/aileron-pid</property></abs>
          <value>0.98</value>
        </gt>
        <value>1.0</value>
        <value>0.0</value>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <fcs_function name="ap/lat/aileron-command">
    <function>
      <sum>
        <property>ap/trim/aileron</property>
        <product>
          <property>ap/sign/aileron</property>
          <property>ap/lat/aileron-pid</property>
        </product>
      </sum>
    </function>
    <clipto> <min> -1.0 </min> <max> 1.0 </max> </clipto>
  </fcs_function>

  <switch name="ap/output/aileron">
    <default value="fcs/aileron-cmd-norm"/>
    <test logic="AND" value="ap/lat/aileron-command">
      ap/enable GT 0.5
    </test>
    <output> fcs/aileron-cmd-norm </output>
  </switch>

</channel>

<!-- ===================================================================
     7. Yaw damper

     A washout on yaw rate, so it damps Dutch roll without fighting the
     steady yaw rate of an intentional turn (§6.4).
     =================================================================== -->
<channel name="Yaw damper" execrate="1">

  <washout_filter name="ap/yaw/r-washout">
    <input> velocities/r-rad_sec </input>
    <c1> ap/yaw/washout-c1 </c1>
  </washout_filter>

  <!-- Turn coordination (Package G): sideslip to rudder. The gains carry
       their own sign (measured: the slope of steady beta against a rudder
       offset on this airframe at this state), so the term needs no
       ap/sign/rudder factor. The integrator is frozen when the rudder
       command sits on a stop, and reset while the autopilot is off. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/yaw/beta-trigger">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <lt><property>ap/enable</property><value>0.5</value></lt>
        <value>-1.0</value>
        <property>ap/yaw/rudder-saturated</property>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <pid name="ap/yaw/beta-pid">
    <input> aero/beta-deg </input>
    <kp> ap/yaw/k-beta </kp>
    <ki> ap/yaw/ki-beta </ki>
    <kd> 0.0 </kd>
    <trigger> ap/yaw/beta-trigger </trigger>
    <clipto> <min> -0.3 </min> <max> 0.3 </max> </clipto>
  </pid>

  <!-- Oppose the washed=out yaw rate. The command is built in the natural
       direction (negative rate demand against positive r) and the airframe's
       measured sign is applied last. -->
  <fcs_function name="ap/yaw/rudder-raw">
    <function>
      <sum>
        <property>ap/trim/rudder</property>
        <product>
          <value>-1.0</value>
          <property>ap/sign/rudder</property>
          <property>ap/yaw/k-r</property>
          <property>ap/yaw/r-washout</property>
        </product>
        <property>ap/yaw/beta-pid</property>
        <property>ap/yaw/rudder-bias</property>
      </sum>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <fcs_function name="ap/yaw/rudder-saturated">
    <function>
      <ifthen>
        <gt><abs><property>ap/yaw/rudder-raw</property></abs><value>0.5</value></gt>
        <value>1.0</value>
        <value>0.0</value>
      </ifthen>
    </function>
  </fcs_function>

  <fcs_function name="ap/yaw/rudder-command">
    <function>
      <property>ap/yaw/rudder-raw</property>
    </function>
    <clipto> <min> -0.5 </min> <max> 0.5 </max> </clipto>
  </fcs_function>

  <switch name="ap/output/rudder">
    <default value="fcs/rudder-cmd-norm"/>
    <test logic="AND" value="ap/yaw/rudder-command">
      ap/enable GT 0.5
    </test>
    <output> fcs/rudder-cmd-norm </output>
  </switch>

</channel>

</system>
